

# 1. Basis of calculation (clearly defined)

Compound (already agreed and correct):

[
\mathrm{C_4H_4N_{12}O_{12}}
]

Molar mass:

[
M_\text{compound} = 412.2\ \text{g·mol}^{-1}
]

Assumption (standard in thermochemistry):

* C → CO₂ (gas)
* H → H₂O (gas)
* N → N₂ (gas)
* Excess O remains as O₂ (gas)

This is **normal combustion / decomposition accounting**, not detonation.

---

# 2. Step-by-step gas stoichiometry → **N (mol gas / g)**

## Step 1: Write atom balances

From **1 mole** of compound:

* C = 4 → 4 CO₂
* H = 4 → 2 H₂O
* N = 12 → 6 N₂
* O present = 12
* O required:

  * CO₂: 4 × 2 = 8 O
  * H₂O: 2 × 1 = 2 O
  * Total = 10 O
* Excess oxygen = 12 − 10 = 2 O → 1 O₂

---

## Step 2: Count gaseous product moles (per mole compound)

| Species | Moles |
| ------- | ----: |
| CO₂     |     4 |
| H₂O     |     2 |
| N₂      |     6 |
| O₂      |     1 |

### Total gas moles:

[
n_\text{gas} = 4 + 2 + 6 + 1 = 13\ \text{mol gas/mol compound}
]

---

## Step :3 Convert to **mol gas per gram**

[
N = \frac{13}{412.2}
]

[
\boxed{N = 0.0315\ \text{mol·g}^{-1}}
]

### ✅ Correct value



---

# 3. Average molar mass of gases → **M**

Now compute **average molar mass of the gaseous mixture**.

## Step 1: Weighted molar masses

| Gas | Moles | Molar mass (g/mol) | Contribution |
| --- | ----: | -----------------: | -----------: |
| CO₂ |     4 |              44.01 |        176.0 |
| H₂O |     2 |              18.02 |         36.0 |
| N₂  |     6 |              28.01 |        168.1 |
| O₂  |     1 |              32.00 |         32.0 |

Total mass of gases:

[
412.1\ \text{g (checks mass balance)}
]

---

## Step 2: Average molecular weight

[
M_\text{avg} = \frac{412.1}{13}
]

[
\boxed{M = 31.7\ \text{g·mol}^{-1}}
]

### ✅ Correct value


---

# 4. Heat released per gram → **Q**

This is **standard heat of combustion**, calculated from heats of formation.

## Standard heats of formation (kcal/mol)

| Species |   ΔH°f |
| ------- | -----: |
| CO₂ (g) | −94.05 |
| H₂O (g) | −57.80 |
| N₂ (g)  |      0 |
| O₂ (g)  |      0 |

Estimated ΔH°f of compound (from group additivity, earlier derived):

[
\Delta H_f^\circ (\text{compound}) \approx +210\ \text{kcal/mol}
]

---

## Step 1: Products enthalpy

[
4(-94.05) + 2(-57.80) = -491.8\ \text{kcal}
]

---

## Step 2: Reaction enthalpy

[
\Delta H = (-491.8) - (+210)
]

[
\Delta H = -701.8\ \text{kcal/mol}
]

---

## Step 3: Convert to **cal/g**

[
Q = \frac{701.8 \times 1000}{412.2}
]

[
\boxed{Q = 1,!700\ \text{cal·g}^{-1}}
]

### ✅ Correct order of magnitude



## Final statement (clean)

> When calculated strictly from molecular stoichiometry and standard thermochemistry, the correct values for C₄H₄N₁₂O₁₂ are:
> **N ≈ 0.0315 mol·g⁻¹**, **M ≈ 31.7 g·mol⁻¹**, and **Q ≈ 1.7 kcal·g⁻¹**.

